# Naming and Single-Responsibility Audit: Scope Pollution and Single Responsibility
File: openOODA/audits/naming/mcode/scope_pollution.oot

Provenance: converted from `openOODA/audits/naming-audit-mcode.md` (788 lines). Part 3 of 7.

Redaction note: fleet host names and tailnet addresses are written as generic
placeholders. The audited tree still holds the literals.

## 3. Defect class (b) — Scope pollution / Unix single-responsibility

### 3.1 `openOODA-apps/oomedia` → `ooflash` — the reference case, now resolved (F9)

**Pre-split state (`oomedia/README.md:3`, captured 03:55 HST):**
> "**Media player with playlist ring + Fedora-style bootable image flasher**"

Measured dominance:

| Cluster | Pages | `.oo` lines | Functions |
|---|---|---|---|
| Flasher domain — `flash/{devlock,digest,catalog,imagew,restore,ui}.oo` | 6 | 332 | 24 |
| Flasher CLI — `personas/oomedia/{verbs,ops}.oo` | 2 | 141 | 11 |
| Flasher tests — `tests/flash/test_flash.oo` | 1 | 140 | 8 |
| **Flasher subtotal** | **9** | **613** | **43** |
| Media-player domain — `probe.oo` + `core/state.oo` + `ui/view.oo` | 3 | 142 | 2 |
| Generic harness (`core/*`, `ipc/*`, `sec/*`, `personas/{multicall,harness}`, `tests/test_*`) | 27 | 1 645 | — |

**The flasher dominated by 4.3:1 on domain lines and ≈21:1 on domain-specific functions.** The "media player" was 23 lines of
extension-sniffing plus a generic ring shared structurally with every sibling repo.

Corroborating smell: `oomedia/ooda.pkg:9` declared `caps=FrameCap,FsReadCap,FsWriteCap,ProcessCap,TcpCap` — the `TcpCap` existed
**only** for the flasher's catalog fetch (`oomedia/flash/catalog.oo:35`). A media player has no business holding a network
capability.

**Post-split state (verified 04:0x HST):**
- `openOODA-apps/ooflash/` exists with `Makefile` (`BIN := bin/ooflash`, `PERSONAS := ooflash`, `VER := 0.1.0`), `README.md`,
  `VERSION`, `ooda.pkg`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`.
- `oomedia/flash/`, `oomedia/personas/oomedia/{verbs,ops}.oo`, `oomedia/tests/flash/test_flash.oo` are deleted.
- `oomedia/Makefile` `SRC` no longer lists any flash page — the dangling `SRC` prerequisites and the dangling
  `import "tests/flash/test_flash.oo";` that existed transiently during the split have both been resolved.
- `ooflash` is a **strict superset** of the old flasher: it adds `flash/rawio.oo` (97 lines, new — bare-metal raw block I/O) and
  `tests/test_raw.oo` (78 lines, new).

**Verdict: SPLIT — correct, and the canonical name `ooflash` is right** (verb-named, not artifact-named; no collision with
`ooimage`).

**But `ooflash` introduces a new standards defect.** `openOODA-apps/ooflash/flash/rawio.oo` shells out to **GNU coreutils
binaries**:

| `ooflash/flash/rawio.oo` | Exec |
|---|---|
| `:21` | `sys_exec(sys, "sha256sum", path)` |
| `:35,37` | `sys_exec(sys, "dd", "if=" + img, "of=" + dpath, "bs=1M", "oflag=direct", "conv=fsync", "status=none")` |
| `:43` | `sys_exec(sys, "sync")` |
| `:50,52` | `sys_exec(sys, "dd", "if=/dev/zero", …)` |
| `:59,61` | `sys_exec(sys, "dd", "if=" + dpath, "of=" + tmp, …)` |
| `:73` | `sys_exec(sys, "truncate", "-s", …, tmp)` |

`ooflash/flash/rawio.oo:3` describes itself as a "**dd-delegated** raw device pipeline" — delegation to a C binary.
`ooflash/flash/digest.oo:8,44-47` adds a size-based "digest road pick": in-process `crypto_sha256_internal` up to 512 MiB,
`sha256sum` above.

This contradicts:
- **Rule 1.25 / the Zero-Legacy-Wrapper standard** — the project standard requires pure native `.oo` compiled via `oodac` to
  ELF64/wasm32, with zero C/Rust/Python fallbacks.
- **`roadmap.oot:90`** — "2026-11: Deprecate non-sandboxed subprocess execution shims across all tooling." New code introduced
  on 2026-11-05 adds exactly such a shim, in the same month the roadmap item is scheduled to land.
- **The `oohash`/`oob3sum` persona surface** — `sha256sum` is already a persona token of the digest host. `ooflash` therefore
  introduces a *third* owner of the `sha256sum` token (see §4.2).

`ooflash` also ships `ooflash/lint-oo-size`, a `/bin/sh` script, whereas the sibling repos implement the same Page-Rule linter
inline in their Makefile and ban `.sh` outside `install.sh`/`uninstall.sh`.

**Verdict on `ooflash`: KEEP the repo and the name; REPLACE the `dd`/`truncate`/`sha256sum`/`sync` execs with native `.oo`
implementations** (O_DIRECT + `fsync` are reachable through the existing `FsWriteCap`/`ProcessCap` surface; `sha256sum` is
already implemented natively by `oohash`/`oob3sum` and by `openOODA-tools/oosha256`).

### 3.2 `openOODA-core/std` — stdlib carrying a kernel stack (F5)
See §2.1. 112 `.oo` files of driver code in a library repo, with two verbatim-identical capability-space pages against
`openOODA-kernel`.

### 3.3 `openOODA-kernel` — 17 repos, one implementation (F7)

Every kernel repo carries the same 15 cross-cutting pages: `cap_reg64.oo`, `wire.oo`, `warrant.oo`, `attest.oo`, `chain.oo`,
`quorum/*`, `speculate/*`, `delegate/*`, `revoke/*`, `proof/*`, `heal/*`, plus a `core/{engine,cap,ring,table}` quartet.
Verified by diff: `oodrv-net/cap_reg64.oo` and `oodrv-pci/cap_reg64.oo` differ **only** in the repo-name token (lines 1 and 3).

The per-repo domain delta is 4 files — `core/{domain}_cap.oo`, `core/{domain}_ring.oo`, `core/{domain}_table.oo`,
`core/{domain}_parse.oo` — and these are themselves name-substituted clones (`drv_net_ring.oo` vs `drv_nvme_ring.oo` differ only
in type/function names and one logline word, "packets" vs "NVMe commands").

**This is Fowler "Duplicated Code" + "Shotgun Surgery" at fleet scale:** a fix to the capability register must be applied in 17
repos.

### 3.4 `openOODA-kernel/ookernel-mm` — a memory manager hosting a third-party anti-cheat gate

`ookernel-mm/iommu/bedaisy_gate.oo:1-3` — "ookernel-mm BEDaisy.sys & Tiger Engine DX11/DX12 IOMMU Compatibility Gate … proving
the IOMMU exposes the DMA translation, interrupt-remapping, and shared-virtual-memory surface that **BattlEye's BEDaisy.sys and
the Destiny 2 Tiger Engine require**." The same gate concept is duplicated at `ookernel-ob/nt/{reqs,gate,render}.oo`.

A memory-manager repo must not carry a BattlEye/anti-cheat compatibility gate, and the gate is duplicated across two repos.
**Verdict: extract to `ookernel-ob/nt/` before archiving `ookernel-mm`.**

### 3.5 `openOODA-agents/oorouter` — an L7 agent router carrying L6 GPU infrastructure

`oorouter/core/gpu_schedule.oo:3` — "Selects healthy GPU nodes for capability-masked inference tasks with fail-closed drops."
`oorouter/core/fleet.oo:3` — "Routes inference tasks across the local 4-GPU Ollama fleet using health-probe admission."

The L6 canonical owner is `openOODA-cloud/ooinfer-grid/core/fleet.oo:3` — "Declares the local 4-GPU inference fleet with
per-node masks and load" — and `ooinfer-grid/core/route.oo:3` — "Routes inference tasks to the lowest-load policy-admitted
healthy GPU with fail-closed drops."

Same 4-GPU fleet, same admission, same lowest-load routing, implemented twice across a layer boundary. **Verdict: delete
`oorouter/core/fleet.oo` and `oorouter/core/gpu_schedule.oo`.**

### 3.6 `openOODA-agents/ooherdr` — four domains in one binary

`ooherdr/core/herd.oo:3` (roster dispatch) + `persona/cluster.oo:3` (3-node cluster failover) + `persona/driver.oo:3` ("external
CLI drivers (devin, mcode, vibe, grok, agy, oopi)") + `persona/oomux.oo:3` ("Multi-pane terminal layout manager for 5-pane
builder, 5-pane reviewer, and experiment nodes").

A terminal multiplexer UI and a hardcoded vendor-driver registry inside the Layer-7 autonomous-agent repo. **Verdict: SPLIT** —
extract `oomux` (TUI) and the driver registry.

### 3.7 `openOODA-apps` — the shared app framework is copy-pasted into every repo

Each of the 16 apps repos carries its own private copy of the same framework pages: `core/engine.oo`, `core/state.oo`,
`core/persist.oo`, `core/capreg.oo`, `core/quorum.oo`, `core/share.oo`, `core/blackbox.oo`, `core/speculate.oo`, `ui/view.oo`,
`ipc/wayland_client.oo`, `ipc/payload.oo`, `ipc/framebuf.oo`, `sec/pqcseal.oo`, `sec/audit.oo`, `sec/delegate.oo`,
`sec/revoke.oo`, plus `tests/test_{pqc,audit,quorum,blackbox,speculate,delegate,revoke}.oo`.

Measured: `core/capreg.oo` is 43 lines in every repo, `ipc/wayland_client.oo` 54 lines, `sec/pqcseal.oo` 95 lines,
`tests/test_main.oo` 112–113 lines — with distinct hashes per repo (i.e. hand-renamed copies, not one shared library).

This is exactly the duplication RFC 0012 §1 identifies as its second structural cause ("1,079 tiny `.oo` wrapper pages … are
copied across the fleet instead of being imported from `openOODA-core/std`"). **Verdict: extract to a shared `std/app/…` module
and import it, per Rule 1.25 §3.**

### 3.8 `openOODA-OS/ooinit` — PID 1 + service manager + health watchdog

`ooinit/README.md:68-70` and `ooinit/Makefile:9` compile `svcmgr/*.oo` (8 pages) and `healthd/*.oo` (8 pages) into the PID-1
binary alongside its own core. Lower severity than `ooimage` because all three share the `ProcessCap` supervision domain, but
`healthd/` is separable. **Verdict: SPLIT candidate** — keep `oosvc-mgr` inside `ooinit`, split `oohealthd` out.

### 3.9 `openOODA-core/cli` — language driver + package installer + self-updater

`cli/README.md:36-52` lists 17 verbs across four domains: language (`build`/`run`/`test`/`fmt`), packaging (`install`),
self-update (`update`/`upgrade`/`update --bootstrap`), scaffolding (`init`), telemetry (`token`), meta (`qa`/`context`/`fix`/
`spec`). `cli/fix/fix.oo:21` is a 170-line function. **Verdict: KEEP** — the breadth is the CLI's stated job — but the `fix`
verb is a large, separable domain.

### 3.10 `openOODA-tools` — the tools layer is un-consolidated

`openOODA-tools` holds ~257 micro-repos, each with `BIN := dist/<repo-name>`. **No Makefile in the entire repo declares a
`PERSONAS :=` variable.** The only persona tables live in `qa/parity/personas.oot`, and exactly **8** repos have one: `oogzip`,
`oohash`, `oob3sum`, `oocert`, `oo7z`, `oovault`, `ooxxd`, `ootar`.

The tools layer therefore has 249 tools with no persona surface at all, while RFC 0012 §2.3 targets **257 → 64 canonical
multi-call utilities**. The gap is 193 tools with no declared canonical host.

Furthermore, those 8 persona tables **relay to system C binaries**:

- `oohash/ipc/dispatch_persona.oo:17-25` maps `sha256sum`/`sha512sum`/`sha1sum`/`sha224sum`/`sha384sum`/`b2sum`/`md5sum`/`cksum`
  /`xxhsum` to upstream names; `:49-61` resolves them in `/usr/local/bin`, `/usr/bin`, `/bin`, `/usr/local/sbin`, `/usr/sbin`,
  `/sbin`; `:64-78` `exec`s them.
- `oohash/qa/parity/personas.oot` holds 24 parity cases against GNU coreutils.
- `oo7z/qa/parity/personas.oot` holds 16 cases against `zip`/`unzip`/`zipinfo`.
- `oogzip/qa/parity/personas.oot` holds 24 cases against `gzip`/`xz`/`zstd`.
- `ooxxd/qa/parity/personas.oot` holds 8 cases against `hexdump`/`od`.
- `ootar/qa/parity/personas.oot` holds 8 cases against `cpio`/`ar`.
- `oocert/qa/parity/personas.oot` holds 9 cases against `openssl`/`trust`.
- `oovault/qa/parity/personas.oot` holds 5 cases against `gpg`.

**This is the single largest Rule-1.25 exposure in the fleet**: the canonical consolidated tools of Layer 3 do not implement
their own persona modes — they `exec` GNU coreutils, OpenSSL and GPG. `oohash` in particular advertises itself as a "Sovereign
Multi-Algorithm Cryptographic Digest Engine" (`oohash/Makefile:2`) while its `sha256`/`sha512`/`md5`/`cksum` personas are `exec`
calls into `/usr/bin`.

**Verdict: this is a native-implementation debt, not a naming defect** — but it directly determines the archive/keep map,
because a tool cannot be declared "absorbed" until its persona is natively implemented and parity-tested without the upstream
binary present.

### 3.11 `openOODA-cloud/oobuild-fleet` ↔ `oodeploy` — mutual absorption cycle

Both repos declare each other as a persona, and both physical subtrees exist:
- `openOODA-cloud/oobuild-fleet/Makefile:24` — `built $(BIN) (--persona personas: oodeploy)`
- `openOODA-cloud/oodeploy/Makefile:25` — `built $(BIN) (--persona personas: oobuild-fleet, ooinfer-grid, oosched-cloud)`
- `oobuild-fleet/ooda.pkg:8` — "absorbs oodeploy into one sys_argv0 multi-call binary"
- `oodeploy/ooda.pkg:8` — "absorbs oobuild-fleet, ooinfer-grid, and oosched-cloud into one sys_argv0 multi-call binary"

Two canonical binaries for one domain, and the two drag in *different* third-party subtrees. `oobuild-fleet` additionally
carries a stale `oobuild-fleet/etc/oodeploy.conf` — the absorbed sibling's config, with no `oobuild-fleet.conf` of its own.

**Verdict: break the cycle.** One host owns build+deploy+infer+sched; the other is archived.
